@cc [owner:id13,label:architecture] independent-distributed-implementation
The FoundationDB implementation and its artifacts MUST stay within dfs-fdb. It MUST NOT instantiate the sibling DFS Engine or RocksDB Store. Frontend correctness MUST NOT depend on exclusive process ownership of a namespace or on a process-local writer lock. Cloud resources MUST belong to dust-dev. Builds, correctness checks, and benchmarks MUST run on GCP; local work is limited to editing, orchestration, and evidence review.

@cc [owner:id13,label:backend;concurrency] atomic-transaction-publication
Filesystem records MUST be published together in one native serializable FoundationDB transaction. Original point and range dependencies, including absence checks and write fences, MUST participate in conflict detection. A definite conflict MUST leave competing changes intact and require revalidation. A commit timeout or possibly committed error MUST remain ambiguous. No publication MAY depend on frontend identity. The v2 direct-record layout MUST use a distinct physical prefix from the former immutable-tree layout; no implicit migration is permitted.

@cc [owner:id13,label:backend;security] immutable-object-integrity
Every immutable object read from storage MUST match its content digest and configured size limit before it enters the cache or is decoded. Missing referenced objects and malformed metadata MUST fail explicitly. A key digest collision MUST NOT silently replace different staged bytes. Object addresses MUST NOT grant callers authority to read their contents.

@cc [owner:id13,label:performance] bounded-storage-working-state
Resident object-cache charges MUST remain within the configured capacity. A local publication batch MUST bound staged objects, mutation count, key/value sizes, and total staged bytes. Read dependency bytes, returned scan bytes, scan item count, outstanding FoundationDB operations, and operation wait time MUST have explicit bounds. Cache capacity MUST NOT be claimed to bound process RSS or caller-retained values. No storage snapshot MAY require a full tenant materialization or full-history replay.

@cc [owner:id13,label:backend;concurrency] stable-storage-snapshots
Each operation MUST use one fixed native FoundationDB read version. Reads and prefix scans MUST NOT silently switch versions, including during retry. A snapshot older than four seconds MUST fail explicitly; callers requiring another attempt MUST revalidate all original preconditions in a new transaction. Prefix scans MUST preserve byte ordering, exclude the continuation key, and remain within the requested prefix. Converting a snapshot to a writable batch MUST preserve its already observed point and range dependencies. Independent batches created from one snapshot MUST NOT alter each other's staged state.

@cc [owner:id13,label:testing] distributed-evidence
Concurrency claims MUST be verified against real FoundationDB using independent clients and caches. HA claims additionally require independent frontend processes and observed failure tests. Storage-level tests MUST NOT be represented as proof of filesystem, authorization, indexing, or routing correctness. Performance evidence MUST state durability, cache conditions, workload, and topology.

@cc [owner:id13,label:api] shared-wire-compatibility
The serialized filesystem model and enum tags MUST preserve existing request identities and payload interpretation. Call and Snapshot RPCs MUST retain their wire formats. The Watch RPC MUST be absent under the one-second demand-refresh consistency model. Protocol source snapshots MUST have recorded fingerprints. Storage implementation changes MUST NOT introduce a dependency on the sibling server engine.

@cc [owner:id13,label:performance;concurrency] one-second-cache-freshness
A client's observable filesystem state, including names, missing entries, attributes, permissions, file size and file contents through already-open descriptors, MUST require authoritative validation once its validation window reaches one second. Operations MAY share validated state and cached bytes within that window. Cache hits, delayed replies, failed refreshes and metadata-only accesses MUST NOT extend an older content or authority deadline. Kernel replies MUST use only the remaining monotonic request-start validation lifetime. Expired state MUST NOT be served on refresh failure. Retaining immutable bytes beyond the window is permitted only when the selected generation and authority are revalidated before reuse. This deadline applies to filesystem calls, not bytes already mapped into application memory: mmap is not a live view. Supported and unsupported mapping modes MUST be explicit.

@cc [owner:id13,label:architecture;concurrency] demand-refresh-without-invalidation-streams
The FoundationDB frontend, router, and mount MUST NOT implement a remote cache-invalidation subscription, Watch RPC, or background head-polling invalidation loop. Remote validation MUST be driven by demand after expiry and MAY be shared or batched across operations. Local kernel-cache expiry or eviction MAY run without network traffic to enforce the shared deadline. Local service shutdown signals MUST NOT transport filesystem changes.

@cc [owner:id13,label:backend;concurrency] coherent-object-generations
Each published file generation MUST atomically bind its size, attributes, manifest, and referenced blocks. A served FUSE read request MUST NOT combine blocks from different file generations or use another generation's EOF. An application operation spanning several FUSE requests is not promised one atomic file snapshot. Directory enumeration MUST use a coherent directory snapshot with stable continuation. Kernel and daemon cache installation MUST preserve those object-generation boundaries. No ordering or winner preference across concurrent clients is promised; a later operation MAY observe a competing client's coherent version.

@cc [owner:id13,label:security;concurrency] shared-session-authority
Authenticated filesystem operations MUST load session validity, current credential authority, and filesystem policy from one authoritative tenant snapshot. Handles and retained view pins MUST bind the same authenticated session and node and MUST remain usable through another frontend. Retained content, sessions, pins, and receipts MUST NOT bypass current authority. Logout MUST remove shared session validity so operations beginning after logout publication fail on every frontend.

@cc [owner:id13,label:backend;concurrency] mounted-live-file-generations
An open descriptor MUST follow the current generation of its stable file identity after its validation window expires; it MUST NOT freeze content until close. Each individual served read MUST bind bytes and EOF to one coherent generation. Separate operations MAY observe different generations. Rename-over and unlink MUST preserve the original open file identity, not redirect an existing descriptor to the new pathname target; current link state and authority still require validation. Writable descriptors MUST observe acknowledged changes without waiting an extra cache window. Competing writes MAY fail on a stale base, with no writer priority. Ambiguous publication retries MUST retain the original publication identity and payload.

@cc [owner:id13,label:concurrency;error-handling] mounted-request-lifecycle
Graceful mount shutdown MUST detach the filesystem and join its FUSE request thread before destroying the async runtime used by callbacks. Retained application descriptors MAY delay the join until closed. A callback-thread panic or shutdown failure MUST NOT be silently reported as clean shutdown.

@cc [owner:id13,label:backend;concurrency] conditional-filesystem-publication
Filesystem publication MUST atomically include node/entry/content changes, journal and indexing events, and the original request's outcome and receipt. Retrying an internal transaction MUST revalidate original client preconditions and current authority. Matching retained requests MUST resolve without republishing. Mismatched payloads MUST fail. Directory moves MUST prevent cycles, stale entry replacement, and nonempty directory replacement across concurrent frontends.

@cc [owner:id13,label:architecture;performance;security] soft-cache-affinity
Routing preference MUST depend only on advisory cache/load/health information and MUST NOT confer tenant ownership or authorization. Retried mutations MUST retain their original serialized request identity and payload. A failed frontend MUST be bypassable by another frontend with the same authoritative namespace. Cache inventory hints MUST be bounded and expire; unknown tenants MUST NOT grow routing state. Frontend cache accounting MUST attribute resident charges to tenants, including eviction. Non-loopback client and frontend transport MUST use TLS.

@cc [owner:id13,label:backend;concurrency] incremental-index-publication
Index workers MUST recover progress from a shared tenant checkpoint bound to the Elasticsearch index UUID. Advancing a checkpoint requires acknowledged, search-visible writes for every affected node through that checkpoint. Ordinary file edits MUST NOT require scanning or rebuilding the namespace. Concurrent or retried workers MUST NOT overwrite a document with an older filesystem snapshot. Deletions MUST retain a versioned tombstone so delayed workers cannot resurrect a removed document. Journal gaps MUST fail explicitly. Index progress MUST NOT require exclusive process ownership.

@cc [owner:id13,label:security] search-authority
Elasticsearch records MUST NOT grant filesystem authority. Every returned search candidate and its content version MUST be validated against current FoundationDB credential authority, session validity when a session is used, and policy. Indexed ancestor paths or inherited ACLs MUST NOT be used as authoritative decisions after moves or policy changes. Index extraction requires an unscoped administrator. Indexing failures MUST NOT roll back filesystem publication or be reported as successful indexing.

@cc [owner:id13,label:security;api] bounded-authorized-search
Search pagination MUST count authorized, current-version matches before applying offset and limit. A query MUST use one fixed-version FoundationDB policy snapshot and one Elasticsearch point-in-time; changes to source head or authority before final validation MUST suppress rows and mark the response incomplete. Index lag, stale candidates, and exhausted candidate bounds MUST NOT be represented as complete results. Token-authenticated HTTP reads MUST NOT create persistent filesystem sessions or require a frontend owner. Query work, response bytes, and admission MUST have explicit bounds; backend failures MUST NOT become successful empty search results.

@cc [owner:id13,label:testing;performance] filesystem-comparison-evidence
Filesystem comparisons MUST execute and validate all requested workload rows on every compared implementation and backend against the same corpus manifest. Result validation and observation collection MUST remain outside timed actions. Reports MUST record actual binaries, source and corpus identities, phase/repetition definitions, mount/cache settings, durability semantics, placement, and raw samples. Earlier measurements or partial failed suites MUST NOT be presented as current successful results. Benchmark scratch writes MUST remain isolated and temporary mounts/services MUST be cleaned up.

@cc [owner:id13,label:backend] foundationdb-chunk-limits
Logical values up to one MiB MUST round-trip through checksummed inline records or ordered chunks no larger than 64 KiB. Missing, oversized, reordered, or corrupt chunks MUST fail before caching or decoding. The logical envelope and all newly referenced chunks MUST commit atomically. Staged mutation charges MUST be bounded by four MiB and recorded read dependencies by one MiB, below FoundationDB's native transaction limit; larger publications MUST fail without partial publication. Cached immutable bytes MUST be selected through an envelope read at the operation's native read version.

@cc [owner:id13,label:concurrency] foundationdb-network-lifetime
Every process using Store MUST call the re-exported unsafe boot function once before connecting. The returned network guard MUST outlive every database, transaction and async task, and MUST be dropped before normal process exit. Process-exit callbacks MUST NOT be used for network shutdown. The network MUST NOT restart after shutdown.

@cc [owner:id13,label:backend;concurrency] optimistic-cache-publication
An expired cached revision MAY be attempted as a conditional write precondition without a preflight fetch; the server MUST still validate original dependencies atomically, and no expired observation MAY be served by read/stat. A successful local mutation MAY overlay its acknowledged file revision only when the cached original base still matches and the view has not advanced beyond that outcome. Such overlays MUST NOT advance the contiguous journal cursor or renew any validation deadline. Definite conflicts MUST invalidate observations without silently rebasing the original payload. Uncertain outcomes MUST retain their original request identity and payload in bounded mount-owned state across descriptor closure.

@cc [owner:id13,label:backend;error-handling] remembered-publication-confirmation
Close-time flush MUST report retained publication errors without requiring still-buffered changes to publish or repeating an explicit fsync durability confirmation. Explicit fsync MUST resolve relevant uncertain outcomes and confirm unconfirmed receipts. Confirmations MAY retire receipts through their confirmed tenant head only within the same server incarnation. Confirmation MUST NOT extend metadata or authority validity. Receipt and pending-request capacity MUST be admitted before publication.

@cc [owner:id13,label:performance;concurrency] asynchronous-mount-content
Network content reads MUST execute outside the FUSE dispatcher with bounded admission. Each reply MUST revalidate its selected revision and authority after fetching. Shutdown MUST drain admitted replies before destroying their async runtime.

@cc [owner:id13,label:security;concurrency] bounded-readdirplus-and-cached-reads
Readdirplus MUST use current authorized attributes with an encoded TTL covered by an authoritative validation deadline and account for every kernel lookup reference actually returned. Extending that deadline during reply construction requires authoritative revalidation of identical encoded attributes and a new deadline covering the encoded TTL; changed attributes MUST discard the reply and release its lookup references. Refreshing directory continuation MUST refresh its attributes, not only its deadline. Synchronous cached reads MUST validate identity, revision and authority under the metadata deadline before replying; misses, expiry and concurrent validation MUST fall back to the bounded asynchronous path without serving expired data.

@cc [owner:id13,label:security;performance;concurrency] authorized-content-hash-cache
Manifest entries MUST bind a file revision and range; content-hashed bytes MUST remain private to the authenticated mount cache and MUST NOT confer authority. Batch reads MUST authorize every file/retained handle from one backend snapshot, bound request count and expanded chunk bytes, and isolate each range's error. Known hashes MAY suppress payload only for that authorized manifest. Clients MUST retain omitted bytes through the response, verify received digests and response identities/ranges/EOF, and revalidate revision and authority before serving or installing fetched entries. Metadata expiry MUST NOT evict immutable content. Combined content and manifest cache charges MUST stay within the configured content-cache budget; queues and admitted reads MUST remain bounded.

@cc [owner:id13,label:performance;concurrency] bounded-read-backpressure
When asynchronous FUSE content reads fill the configured admission capacity, the dispatcher MUST wait for a completion instead of returning EAGAIN solely because capacity is full. Outstanding daemon read tasks MUST remain bounded by that capacity; excess requests remain in the kernel queue or the dispatcher. Completion and shutdown MUST wake and drain waiters without requiring another FUSE callback.

@cc [owner:id13,label:backend;performance;concurrency] bounded-index-backlog-drain
Index passes MUST range-read at most 1024 contiguous journal events, reject gaps, and coalesce affected node identities. At most eight documents MAY be fetched concurrently, and materialized encoded documents MUST be bounded to 64 MiB. Capacity or source-snapshot deadlines MUST reduce the attempted event batch without skipping uncheckpointed events. Every successful pass with documents MUST acknowledge an Elasticsearch refresh after all bulk writes, including version conflicts, before advancing its shared checkpoint. Existing backlog SHOULD be drained without a fixed sleep between successful passes.

@cc [owner:id13,label:security;performance;backend] independent-index-checkpoint-authority
Index checkpoint updates MUST validate the unscoped administrator's current shared session and credential and compare the expected checkpoint in one transaction. They MUST NOT read or lock the unrelated filesystem tenant head. This does not relax filesystem operation or search-candidate policy checks.

@cc [owner:id13,label:security;performance] indexed-literal-candidates
Literal substring search MUST escape wildcard metacharacters and use an indexed literal candidate predicate, followed by current filesystem authorization and exact literal validation. It MUST NOT enumerate every document and fetch each body merely to locate a rare substring. A changed search mapping MUST select a new index schema identity and re-establish its UUID-bound checkpoint before reporting complete results.

@cc [owner:id13,label:backend;concurrency;error-handling] bounded-buffered-file-publication
Default mounts MAY acknowledge bounded file creation, content and attribute changes before remote publication. Pending bytes MUST remain mount-owned across close, with at most 128 files or directories and two MiB of file payload in a publication batch. Background publication MUST become eligible within 100 ms of the oldest remaining record's original acceptance and apply backpressure while publishing. Edits and retries MUST NOT reset acceptance times; a partial drain MUST remove timestamps only for confirmed records. Explicit fsync and graceful unmount MUST drain accepted changes and confirm durability; failures MUST propagate. Definite rejection MUST retain a deferred failure, and ambiguous retries MUST reuse the sealed identity and payload. Capacity-triggered publication MAY exclude the currently edited file, but the selected subset MUST remain sealed across uncertain retries and synchronization MUST drain both selected and retained files. A batch MUST validate current authority, absent names and identities for creates, and original revision and entry identity for replacements atomically with every file, journal event, index event and receipt. New directories MUST precede their descendants in a sealed batch and inherit only validated parent authority. Directory attribute replacements MUST retain the original revision and entry identity, and directory records MUST carry no file payload. Publication MUST NOT advance a client's contiguous journal cursor. Client metadata and kernel TTLs MUST expire within 500 ms from validation start; cache hits MUST NOT renew authority. Delayed or partitioned publication MUST NOT be described as satisfying a one-second visibility bound from local acknowledgement.

@cc [owner:id13,label:backend;performance;concurrency] bounded-publication-prefetch
Publication prefetch MUST use the transaction's fixed snapshot and preserve native read conflicts or optimistic point locks, including missing keys. It MUST bound input keys and retained values and invalidate prefetched entries on staged writes or deletions. Prefetch MUST NOT confer authority or permit stale data across transactions.

@cc [owner:id13,label:backend;performance;concurrency] bounded-incremental-refresh
A delta within 4096 retained journal events MUST remain incremental unless an authority/reset event requires a new view. Distributed implementations MUST read the contiguous journal range, verify sequence identity, and fetch changed nodes and retained pins in bounded batches. A journal gap or expired source snapshot MUST fail rather than skip events. A local publication receipt MUST NOT alone advance the client's contiguous journal cursor.

@cc [owner:id13,label:backend;security;performance] transaction-key-hints
Session key hints MAY retain only credential and principal key addresses, bounded to 256 entries and 256 KiB of charged key/value storage. Every operation MUST read session validity and current credential authority in its native transaction; a hint MUST NOT authorize an operation or renew metadata. Mutation prefetch MUST retain conflict dependencies on absent and present target keys, original revisions, current authority and retry outcomes. A stale hint MUST fall back to the key selected by the authoritative session record. Index checkpoint-only transactions MUST NOT acquire a filesystem-head dependency.
